---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeTotp from '@/components/tools/security/DevEncodeTotp.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hotp-generator">
  <DevEncodeTotp client:load mode="hotp" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Counter-based, not time-based</h2>
    <p>
      HOTP is the original one-time-password algorithm, standardised as RFC 4226 in 2005. It is the same
      as TOTP with one difference: the counter in the HMAC is a number that goes up when a code is
      generated, and does not come from the clock.
    </p>
    <p>
      So an HOTP generator has no clock to keep in sync and no expiry, but it has its own failure mode.
    </p>

    <h2>HOTP versus TOTP</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>HOTP (RFC 4226)</th><th>TOTP (RFC 6238)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Counter source</td><td>An event counter</td><td>Unix time ÷ step</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Code lifetime</td><td>Until it is used</td><td>Typically 30 seconds</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Needs a clock</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, on both sides</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Main failure mode</td><td>Counter drift</td><td>Clock skew</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Server state</td><td>Must persist the counter</td><td>Stateless beyond the secret</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Hardware tokens with no battery, OATH keys</td><td>Authenticator apps, nearly all web 2FA</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Counter drift, and how servers cope</h2>
    <p>
      Each time a user presses the button on a token and does not submit the code, the token's counter
      moves forward and the server's does not. After enough idle presses the two are far apart, and the
      server rejects all codes.
    </p>
    <p>
      So servers accept a look-ahead window, usually the next 3 to 10 counters. When a submitted code
      matches one of them, the server resynchronises and sets its counter past the match. The verifier
      above does this: it checks ten steps ahead and shows the value to set on the server. A server
      must never accept a counter behind its own, because then an attacker can replay a captured code.
    </p>

    <h2>Which is more secure?</h2>
    <p>
      Each has a weakness. An HOTP code stays valid until used, so a code seen over the shoulder or
      caught by phishing keeps working. A TOTP code expires in under a minute, but TOTP fails completely
      if a device's clock is wrong, and many 2FA support tickets are about that. Most services choose
      TOTP, because clock sync is cheap on a phone and the short window helps.
    </p>

    <h2>Where you still meet HOTP</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>OATH-HOTP hardware tokens: a keyfob with one button and no clock.</li>
      <li>YubiKey OATH-HOTP slots, still used in enterprise deployments.</li>
      <li>Bank card readers that generate a code per transaction.</li>
      <li>Paper backup lists, where each code is the next counter value in sequence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The URI for enrollment</h2>
    <p>
      An HOTP provisioning URI carries <code>counter</code> in place of <code>period</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>otpauth://hotp/Example:alice?secret=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP&amp;counter=0&amp;digits=6</code></pre>
    <p>
      Fewer authenticator apps support the <code>hotp</code> type than <code>totp</code>. Some import it
      as TOTP without a warning, and then their codes never match. Test the first code after you enrol.
    </p>

    <h2>Computed here, stored nowhere</h2>
    <p>
      The HMAC runs in WebCrypto in this tab, and the page does not save the secret or the counter. When
      you refresh the page, both are gone. Use this to test a token, find where a counter ended up, or
      validate an integration. Do not keep a credential here.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
